course: dictionary
lesson: 13384
title: Headwinds: a word from the sky and the sea
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Headwinds are winds that blow towards you as you move, so they slow you down. Picture a cyclist pushing against the wind on a flat road. «The cyclist pushed hard against strong headwinds on the flat road.» The wind is in their face, so they must work harder to go forward.
explain: Business people use headwinds in a different way. They mean problems from outside a company that slow its growth or hurt its sales, just like wind slowing a boat. «The shop had a hard year because of headwinds.» Read the clues: the shop had a hard year, and something outside caused it.
explain: Notice why this word is used. When a company says it faced headwinds, it blames outside forces, not its own mistakes. Ask yourself: did the company say what went wrong, or did it just point at the weather? Then check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence.
fact: Headwind means wind blowing towards you, which slows you down.
fact: In business, «headwinds» means outside problems that slow growth or hurt sales.
fact: The business meaning comes from flying and sailing, where headwinds really do slow things down.
fact: People often say «headwinds» to blame outside forces and avoid admitting their own mistakes.
fact: «Headwinds» is usually used in the plural in the business sense: «the company faced headwinds».
exercise: «The cyclist pushed hard against strong headwinds on the flat road.» What are the headwinds in this sentence? | A steep hill at the end of the road | Wind blowing behind the cyclist and pushing them along | *Wind blowing towards the cyclist and slowing them down | Rain that makes the road slippery
exercise: Ellie reads, «The shop had a hard year because of headwinds.» What might headwinds mean in business? | Long holidays for the staff | *Outside problems that slow growth or hurt sales | Free gifts given to every customer | Very strong winds that knock down shop signs
exercise: Why might a company say «we faced headwinds» instead of «we made mistakes»? | To say that it has the richest boss in town | *To blame outside forces and not admit its own mistakes | Because it wants to sell more kites | To show that it won a sailing race
test: «The plane flew slowly because of strong headwinds.» What was slowing the plane down? | Wind blowing with it, helping it along | A heavy load of lost luggage | Clouds of smoke from the engine | *Wind blowing against it
test: Sam says headwinds is just another word for a windy day. What is the better answer? | Not quite, in business it means problems that slow growth or hurt sales | Yes, it always means a windy day | Yes, it means a day at the seaside | *Not quite, the word is only ever used in business for good news
exam: «The factory's sales fell, and its boss blamed headwinds.» What does the boss most likely mean? | *Problems from outside, which the boss says caused the fall in sales | The staff have been taking too many holidays | The factory has started making kites | The factory has just bought a faster machine
